{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789836682,"nonce":"P6r1pcX/7PxlIm6pOnf9BqWs3aY=","title":"The first iteration of a kernel carries the compile of its hot function","message":"With every body compiled on its call (872b63e), the optimisation and\nCranelift compile of the function that holds a kernel's loop lands\ninside the first timed iteration unless the warm-up thread gets there\nfirst: nbody's first iteration went from 8 to 31 ms, meteor's from 66 to\n100, fannkuch's from 38 to 54 (NUC). Over the speed center's 50\niterations that is +0.5 ms on nbody's mean and +1.5 on meteor's, enough\nto lose to PyPy on nbody's mean (7.5 vs 7.1) while the wall drops by\n100 ms; the steady state is unchanged.\n\nWhere the time goes (nbody, ZYNTAX_TRACE_LAZY=1): the warm-up thread\nruns the entry's reachable set in call-graph order, program functions\nbefore the library's, but optparse's parse_args (15 ms to optimise, runs\nonce) and the module body (`__main__`, 15 ms) come before `main` and\n`advance` (13 ms); the interpreter reaches `advance` first, interprets\nits loop for 256 header visits, waits for the compile, transfers into\nthe baseline.\n\nDirections:\n- Order the warm-up by what the interpreter actually enters: the body\n  source hook fires at a function's first run; a queue fed from it puts\n  `advance` at the head the moment `main` calls it.\n- Cheaper per-function optimisation for big functions: the vectorizers\n  and affine pass cost 2.5 of `advance`'s 13 ms and never fire on Python\n  code; three rounds where the second and third change little.\n- A non-blocking interpreter tick: keep interpreting while the compile\n  is in flight instead of waiting at the 20th call.","files":null}]}